# Clean Changes

## Read Before Writing
Before modifying any file, read the entire file — not just the area around the change. Understand what exists before adding anything.

Before creating a new function, search the codebase for existing functions that do something similar. If one exists, modify it — do not create a duplicate.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — wrote generatePost() without checking, didn't notice createPost() already exists
export function generatePost(brief: string) { ... }
export function createPost(brief: string) { ... }  // orphaned, does 90% the same thing

// ✅ GOOD — found createPost(), renamed and extended it
export function generatePost(brief: string) { ... }  // old createPost removed
```

## Replace, Don't Stack
When changing logic, remove what you're replacing. Never leave the old version:
- Sitting below the new version
- Commented out "just in case"
- Renamed with a `_old` or `_backup` suffix
- Still exported but no longer called

If the old code is still needed somewhere, trace every call site and update them. If it's not needed, delete it.

## After Every Change, Clean Up
Before considering a task done, check the files you touched for:
- Unused imports at the top of the file
- Functions that are no longer called by anything
- Variables that are assigned but never read
- Types or interfaces that nothing references
- Commented-out code blocks

Remove all of them. Dead code is not "safe to keep around." It confuses future AI sessions, increases file size, and creates false leads when debugging.

## One Version of Every Piece of Logic
There must never be two functions, two components, or two code paths that do the same thing. If you find a duplicate while working:
- Determine which version is correct
- Delete the other
- Update all call sites

## When Fixing a Bug
Before writing a fix:
1. Read the entire file the bug is in
2. Read the files that call into and out of it
3. Understand the full flow, not just the broken line
4. Write a fix that fits coherently into the existing structure

Never patch a symptom without understanding the cause. A targeted fix in the right place is always better than a workaround bolted on top.
